 flat := make([]object.TreeEntry, 0, len(entries))\n    for _, entry := range entries {                        // Converts map→slice\n        flat = append(flat, entry)\n    }\n    return flat, nil\n}\n```\n\nCalled from `writePendingToCurrent` (line 523), the returned slice is iterated once to insert back into a map (lines 527-532). **Fix:** Return the map directly from `FlattenTree`, or pass the map to `writePendingToCurrent` to avoid the conversion.\n\n### 3. **No Tree Hash Comparison Before Commit — POTENTIAL NO-OP (Low Severity)**\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:556-566` (`writePendingToCurrent`)\n\nAfter building the entries map, a new tree is created and committed. However, there's no check if `treeHash == rootTreeHash` (the existing tree). If all pending entries already exist in `/full/current` with identical hashes, a redundant commit is created.\n\n**Fix:** Add early return:\n```go\nif treeHash == rootTreeHash {\n    return nil  // Tree unchanged, skip commit\n}\n```\n\n### 4. **FALSE POSITIVE — Not an issue:**\nThe \"scan ALL entries per pending raw path\" concern is **not a practical problem** because:\n- `writePendingToCurrent` is called **exactly once** at the end of migration (via `finalize()`), not in a hot loop.\n- Typical migrations batch checkpoints (default ~100 per generation), so entries count stays bounded.\n- Even with 1000 entries and 10 sessions, the 10,000-operation inner loop is acceptable for a one-time migration.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Issue | Location | Severity | Impact |\n|-------|----------|----------|--------|\n| O(entries × paths) matching loop | Lines 539-544 | **Medium** | Scales poorly with large `/full/current` trees over repeated flushes |\n| Map→Slice→Map conversions | Lines 749-759 | Low | Minor allocation waste on every call |\n| No tree hash equality check | Lines 556-566 | Low | Rare no-op commits if pending entries match existing tree |\n\nAll three are fixable with 2-5 line changes. The matching loop